   "stem": {
      "text": "A curve is given in polar coordinates by \\( r = 2 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + 3 \\), where \\( x = r\\cos\\theta \\) and \\( y = r\\sin\\theta \\). At a point P on the curve, the slope of the line tangent to the curve is \\( -4 - \\frac{5 \\sqrt{3}}{3} \\), and the rate of change of y with respect to \\( \\theta \\) is \\( 1 - \\frac{3 \\sqrt{3}}{2} \\). A calculator may be used. Without differentiating the polar equation, find the exact rate of change of x with respect to \\( \\theta \\) at P, and show the work that leads to your answer.",
